{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that delves into the complexities of friendship, ambition, and the consequences of success. directed by david finchercer, the movie tells the story of mark zuckerberg, the co-founder of facebook, and his journey from a talented but awkward college student to a billionaire entrepreneur.\n\nthe film boasts an impressive cast, with jesse eisenberg delivering a standout performance as mark zuckerberg. eisenberg's portrayal of zuckerberg is both captivating and unsettling, capturing the character's intellectual intensity, social awkwardness, and obsessive drive. the supporting cast, including andrew garfield as edgar mascaro and armie hammer as sean parker, also deliver strong performances that add depth and nuance to the film.\n\none of the standout aspects of the social network is its script, which is both witty and poignant. the dialogue is razor-sharp, with clever one-liners and biting observations that capture the essence of the characters and the era in which they lived. the script also tackles complex themes, such as the ethics of social media and the consequences of unchecked ambition.\n\nvisually, the film is stunning, with a blend of warm and cool tones that evoke the nostalgia of the early 2000s. the cinematography is elegant and understated, capturing the beauty of new york city's campus and the intimacy of the characters' relationships. the film's score, composed by trent reznor and attorney, is equally impressive, perfectly capturing the mood and atmosphere of each scene.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the social network is its portrayal of the relationships between the characters. the film shows how even the closest of friends can become embroiled in a web of deceit and betrayal, as zuckerberg's obsession with facebook takes a toll on his relationships with edgar and sean. t